An onion service carries a pancake session over
[Tor](https://www.torproject.org/). A peer is named by a version 3 service id,
the 56 base32 characters in `ocapn://<serviceid>.onion`. Bytes travel a Tor
circuit: encrypted, with both ends' network location hidden.

This is the only transport the OCapN draft standardizes, and the only netlayer
upstream [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) ships. Every
other live Goblins check (cleartext `tcp`, and `iroh`) runs over an adapter
this repository wrote for the vat. Over Tor neither side wrote the other's
transport; both follow the draft. `zig build goblins-onion` is the live check.

# A Tor you already run

A deployment runs a Tor daemon; pancake talks to it. You give the netlayer two
endpoints, each an absolute Unix socket path or a `host:port`:

- the **SOCKS endpoint**, which pancake dials through, and
- the **control endpoint**, which pancake asks for a service when it hosts.

Leave the control endpoint out and the netlayer can dial but not host; it says
so rather than reporting an address nothing can reach. The control endpoint may
want a cookie file's bytes or a password; pancake sends either.

Hosting mints a version 3 service and reports the service id partners dial, plus
the key that restores that address after a restart. Store the key and pass it
back to keep one address across restarts.

# Dial

Point the netlayer at an already-running Tor SOCKS endpoint and name the
service id to reach.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:onion GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **Zig**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
>
> pub fn example(io: std.Io, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !void {
>     // Dial through the same SOCKS endpoint the listener named.
>     var onion_resident = pancake.net.onion.Resident{ .io = io, .config = .{
>         .socks = "/run/tor/socks",
>     } };
>     const netlayers = [_]pancake.net.netlayer.Netlayer{onion_resident.netlayer()};
>     const partner = pancake.net.onion.locator("<56-character onion service id>");
> ```
> **TypeScript**
> ```ts
> import { locator, provider } from "@pancake/onion";
> import { Runtime } from "@pancake/runtime";
>
> // pancake starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> const partner = locator("<56-character onion service id>");
>
> await using runtime = await Runtime.open({
> 	providers: [provider({ socks: "/run/tor/socks" })],
> });
> ```
> **Python**
> ```python
> from pancake import Runtime, onion
>
> # pancake starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> partner = onion.locator("<56-character onion service id>")
>
> async with Runtime.open(
>     provider=onion.provider(onion.OnionConfig(socks="/run/tor/socks"))
> ) as runtime:
> ```
> **Rust**
> ```rust
> use pancake::onion::{self, Config};
> use pancake::{Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // pancake starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> let partner = onion::locator("<56-character onion service id>");
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([onion::provider(
> 	Config::dial_only("/run/tor/socks"),
> )]))
> .await?;
> ```
> **Racket**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt" "pancake-onion.rkt")
>
> ;; pancake starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> (define partner (onion-locator "<56-character onion service id>"))
> (define runtime
>   (open-runtime #:providers
>                 (list (onion-provider #:socks "/run/tor/socks"))))
> ```
